// HARD Question Bank - Domain 3.0 Security Architecture (90)
window.HARD_BANK = (window.HARD_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 3.1 (24) ----------
{o:"3.1",q:"A company runs its customer portal on a PaaS offering. An attacker exploits an SQL injection flaw in the portal's own code and dumps the customer table. Under the shared responsibility model, which party is accountable for remediating the flaw?",c:["The PaaS provider, because it operates the runtime and web tier that processed the malicious request","Both parties equally, because PaaS splits every control 50/50 under the standard service agreement","The customer, because application code and data sit above the layer a PaaS provider manages","The database vendor, because the injected statement was executed by its query engine"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The provider runs the platform that executed the request, but it did not write the vulnerable query. It would be responsible if the flaw were in its managed runtime rather than in customer code.","Incorrect. Responsibility is divided by layer of the stack, not by percentage. Some controls such as identity are shared, but code flaws are not among them.","Correct. In PaaS the provider secures the facility, hardware, hypervisor, OS and runtime, while the customer owns the application code, its configuration and its data. The injection flaw is in the customer's code, so remediation falls to the customer.","Incorrect. The engine did exactly what the submitted SQL instructed. The vendor is responsible only for flaws in the engine itself, such as a product vulnerability needing a vendor patch."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A startup replaces its VM-based API tier with serverless functions triggered through an API gateway. Compared with the VM design, which security concern becomes MORE important for the team to own?",c:["Least-privilege execution roles, validation of event input, and vetting of third-party packages","Guest OS patch cadence, kernel hardening and host-based antivirus on each function's runtime","Hypervisor isolation settings, host firmware updates and virtual switch configuration for each function","Physical access logging, rack-level controls and environmental monitoring for the hosting facility"],a:0,e:["Correct. With serverless the provider handles the OS and runtime, so risk concentrates in what the customer still controls: over-permissioned roles, untrusted event data and packaged libraries.","Incorrect. These are the customer's concerns on IaaS VMs. In serverless the provider patches and hardens the underlying runtime, so this concern shrinks rather than grows.","Incorrect. The hypervisor and virtual networking are provider-managed in any public cloud model, and in serverless the customer cannot even see them. They matter to a private-cloud operator.","Incorrect. Physical and environmental controls belong to the provider in every public cloud model. They would be the customer's concern only in an on-premises or colocation design."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A developer commits an infrastructure-as-code template to a public Git repository. The template contains a live cloud access key. Which response is MOST appropriate as the immediate action?",c:["Re-run the template to redeploy the stack so the infrastructure matches the committed definition","Revoke and rotate the exposed key at once, then purge the history and enable secret scanning","Rewrite the commit history and force-push, since removing the key from the repo ends the exposure","Switch the repository to private and monitor API activity for suspicious calls over the coming weeks"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Redeploying addresses configuration drift but leaves the leaked credential valid. It would be the right step if the concern were an unauthorized manual change to the environment.","Correct. Public keys are harvested by bots within minutes, so the credential must be invalidated first. Purging history and adding pre-commit scanning prevent a repeat.","Incorrect. Forks, clones and caches may already hold the key, so a rewrite alone does not make it safe. History cleanup is a follow-up to rotation, not a substitute for it.","Incorrect. Making the repo private does not undo prior exposure, and waiting leaves a valid key in attackers' hands. Monitoring is useful after the key has been revoked."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An architect compares containers with full virtual machines for hosting mutually untrusted tenants on the same physical host. Which characteristic of containers is the main reason VMs provide stronger isolation here?",c:["Containers cannot be placed on separate network segments, so tenants must share one flat network","Container images cannot be patched in place, so a vulnerable base layer stays exposed indefinitely","Container processes inherit the application owner's credentials, so tenants share a common identity store","Containers share the host kernel, so a kernel-level exploit can cross container boundaries"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Containers support overlay networks, network policies and separate bridges. A network limitation is not what distinguishes their isolation from a VM's.","Incorrect. Images are rebuilt from patched base layers and redeployed. Immutability is a patching workflow, not an isolation weakness, and VMs have their own patching duties.","Incorrect. Containers can run under distinct service accounts and, with rootless mode, as unprivileged users. Identity sharing is a configuration choice, not an inherent property.","Correct. VMs each run their own guest kernel above a hypervisor, while containers rely on namespaces and cgroups over one kernel. A kernel flaw can therefore expose every container on the host."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company wants one central controller that can instantly push a quarantine policy to every switch in its campus when an endpoint is flagged, without logging in to each device. Which architecture provides this?",c:["Software-defined networking with the control plane separated into a central controller","VLAN trunking with an 802.1Q configuration applied on each access and distribution switch","Static routing with manually defined routes and ACLs placed on each layer 3 device","Physical air-gapped segments with separate cabling and no interconnection between zones"],a:0,e:["Correct. SDN decouples the control plane from forwarding hardware, so a controller can program flow rules on all switches at once through its southbound API.","Incorrect. VLANs segment traffic, but each switch still has to be configured individually or through a separate management tool. They suit static logical segmentation instead.","Incorrect. Static routes are fixed by hand and not dynamic. They fit small, stable networks where change is rare, not instant campus-wide response.","Incorrect. An air gap isolates networks physically but cannot be reprogrammed on demand. It suits environments like classified or ICS networks that must never connect."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A water utility operates legacy PLCs that speak Modbus/TCP, which has no authentication, and the devices cannot be updated. Which approach is the MOST effective compensating architecture?",c:["Enable AES-256 encryption in the firmware of each PLC and require certificate authentication per session","Move the PLC network onto the corporate VLAN and install an endpoint agent on each controller","Isolate the PLCs in a zone behind an industrial firewall, with ICS-aware monitoring on the conduit","Set a long, complex shared password on the PLC management port and rotate it every quarter"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Encryption and certificates would solve the issue, but legacy Modbus PLCs lack firmware support and cannot be updated, so this control is not available.","Incorrect. Joining the corporate network increases exposure, and PLCs cannot run endpoint agents. Corporate VLAN placement is for ordinary IT assets.","Correct. Zones, conduits and protocol-aware inspection (Purdue model) compensate for protocol weaknesses that cannot be fixed on the device itself.","Incorrect. Modbus itself has no authentication, so a management password does not protect command traffic. Password rotation fits devices that actually authenticate users."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A medical device manufacturer is selecting an operating system for an infusion pump that must deliver doses on exact schedules. Which consideration MOST strongly drives the choice of an RTOS?",c:["Compatibility with a broad catalog of third-party applications for clinical staff","Guaranteed deterministic response times for safety-critical tasks","A rich graphical interface with multitasking for complex touch workflows","Support for large memory capacity and high-throughput storage for local data analysis"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. App breadth favors general-purpose OSs and widens the attack surface. It would matter for a clinical workstation rather than a dose-delivery controller.","Correct. An RTOS guarantees tasks complete within bounded time, which is essential when a missed deadline could harm a patient.","Incorrect. A rich GUI is not what an RTOS offers and adds complexity. It would be the priority for a consumer or kiosk device.","Incorrect. Embedded pumps are resource-constrained and need predictable timing rather than heavy storage. Large memory suits analytics servers."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company migrates from a centralized on-premises data center to a decentralized multi-cloud design. Which security challenge increases the MOST as a result?",c:["Physical theft or tampering with servers and network gear in the company's own facility","Capacity planning for cooling, power and rack space across the primary data center","Patching of company-owned hypervisors, storage firmware and BIOS across the on-premises server room","Consistent policy, visibility and identity management across several provider environments"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Physical risk to company-owned hardware falls when workloads move to provider facilities. It would remain the main concern for an on-premises design.","Incorrect. Facilities capacity moves to the providers. It is an operational issue for on-premises operators rather than a security challenge that grows in multi-cloud.","Incorrect. In public cloud the provider patches the hypervisor and hardware. This burden shrinks, though it dominates for a private data center.","Correct. Each cloud has different consoles, logging formats and IAM models, so uniform policy and visibility become harder. Central identity and tooling are needed to keep governance consistent."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An architect must justify running a non-critical internal wiki on a single instance instead of a redundant cluster. Which consideration BEST supports that decision?",c:["The business impact of an outage is low compared with the cost of building redundancy","A single instance has a smaller attack surface and is therefore more secure than a cluster","Wiki platforms do not support clustering or replication in any supported configuration","Availability is outside the security triad, so uptime requirements do not affect design choices"],a:0,e:["Correct. Architecture should be risk-based: availability controls are scaled to criticality, and low-impact systems may accept a longer outage rather than pay for redundancy.","Incorrect. Fewer nodes can reduce complexity, but a single instance is not more secure by nature. Security and availability need independent justification.","Incorrect. Many wiki products can be clustered. Inability to cluster would be a technical constraint, not a risk-based justification.","Incorrect. Availability is one of the three CIA objectives. It is weighed against cost here, not dismissed."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which statement BEST describes a security benefit of a well-designed microservices architecture?",c:["Fewer network connections exist between components, which reduces monitoring overhead and attack paths","A shared service account with database-wide rights simplifies auditing and credential rotation across all services","A compromise can be contained to one service when each has a least-privilege identity and API authentication","Internal APIs can omit authentication because they sit behind the perimeter and are not reachable from the internet"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Microservices increase east-west connections between services. A monolith has fewer internal network paths, so this describes the opposite.","Incorrect. A shared, broad credential enlarges the blast radius of any compromise. Distinct least-privilege identities per service are the design goal.","Correct. Smaller, isolated services with scoped identities limit lateral movement and blast radius when well designed.","Incorrect. Internal east-west calls still need mutual authentication, because attackers who land inside move laterally. Zero trust treats internal traffic as untrusted."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"After an incident, a review finds the attacker reached the hypervisor management console from the corporate LAN. Which design principle was MOST clearly violated?",c:["Data minimization of the virtual machine images stored on the hypervisor datastore","Management plane isolation on a dedicated admin network reached through a jump host","Application layer filtering through a WAF placed in front of the console","Certificate pinning on the console so clients accept only the expected server certificate"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Data minimization limits what is collected or stored, not who can reach management interfaces. It matters for privacy compliance rather than console exposure.","Correct. Hypervisor and cloud-management interfaces should sit on isolated admin networks, so a compromised user workstation cannot reach them directly.","Incorrect. A WAF protects web applications from HTTP attacks. The core failure was network reachability, so isolation is what addresses it.","Incorrect. Pinning prevents some man-in-the-middle attacks but does not stop an attacker with credentials or network access from reaching the console."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A power company requires that its SCADA network have no path to the internet or the corporate network, and that telemetry leave only through a hardware device that physically permits traffic in one direction. Which model is this?",c:["Hybrid cloud with an encrypted private circuit to the provider","Logical segmentation using VLANs and ACLs between the SCADA and corporate zones","Zero Trust access through a software-defined perimeter with per-session policy checks","An air gap supplemented by a unidirectional gateway (data diode)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Nothing here involves a cloud provider. A hybrid design would be appropriate if SCADA workloads were extended into a public cloud.","Incorrect. VLANs share hardware and permit bidirectional flows according to configuration. The physical one-way requirement rules this out.","Incorrect. Zero trust uses identity-based verification, whereas the scenario requires physical one-way enforcement. A software-defined perimeter would suit remote workforce access.","Correct. A data diode physically allows one-way flow, so telemetry can be exported while no return path exists for an attacker."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company chooses a cloud region to satisfy data-residency law. Later it finds the provider replicates backups to a region in another country by default. What is the primary issue?",c:["Data sovereignty exposure; the company should restrict backup replication to approved regions","Backup integrity risk; the company should switch to differential backups at the original region","Availability risk; the company should add a second replica in the same region","Encryption risk; the company should rotate the key used to protect the replicated backups"],a:0,e:["Correct. Default replication can move regulated data across borders, so the customer must configure region restrictions and verify them.","Incorrect. Backup type has no bearing on where copies are stored. Differential versus incremental affects restore time and storage size.","Incorrect. Extra same-region replicas improve availability but do not stop cross-border copies. The cross-border replication is the compliance problem.","Incorrect. Key rotation helps with key hygiene, but encryption does not remove jurisdictional obligations over where data is stored. It would address concern over key age."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which of the following is the MOST significant security consideration when deploying large numbers of IoT sensors on an enterprise network?",c:["High power consumption that raises operating costs and cooling demand for the facility","Limited wireless range that requires additional access points and site surveys near the sensors","Weak default credentials, infrequent firmware updates and no support for monitoring agents","Dependence on proprietary licensing and vendor support contracts that raise the cost of replacement"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Power use is an operational and cost concern. It does not create the confidentiality or integrity risk that matters for security.","Incorrect. Range is a deployment and design issue rather than a security flaw. It would matter for coverage planning.","Correct. IoT devices often ship with default passwords, rarely get patched and cannot host endpoint agents, leaving them hard to protect and observe.","Incorrect. Licensing cost is a procurement concern. It does not affect how attackers can compromise the devices."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Analysts use a type 2 hypervisor on laptops to detonate malware samples. Which precaution is MOST important?",c:["Attach the guest to the corporate LAN in bridged mode so threat intelligence feeds and tool updates are reachable","Use host-only networking, disable shared folders and clipboard, and snapshot a clean state to revert to","Mount the host documents directory as a shared folder so samples can be copied in conveniently","Run the hypervisor under a domain administrator account so it can manage virtual devices"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Bridging places a detonating sample directly on the corporate network. A controlled, simulated network or host-only mode is used instead.","Correct. These measures stop the sample from reaching the host, the network or shared data, and the snapshot allows quick restoration to a clean state.","Incorrect. A shared folder gives malware a path to the host file system. Samples should be copied through a controlled one-way transfer instead.","Incorrect. Excess privilege increases impact if the sample escapes. Hypervisors should run with least privilege."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An organization moves a workload from SaaS to IaaS. Which responsibility MOST clearly shifts to the customer?",c:["Physical access control and environmental monitoring for the data center","Hypervisor patching, host firmware management and virtual switch hardening","Power, cooling and network backbone capacity for the hosting facility and its carrier links","Guest OS patching, host firewall rules and application hardening"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The provider keeps physical security in every cloud model. It is the customer's concern only for on-premises or colocation.","Incorrect. The provider manages the hypervisor and host hardware in IaaS. Customers patch guest operating systems instead.","Incorrect. Facilities remain with the provider. These duties would belong to the customer only in a private data center.","Correct. IaaS gives the customer the OS and up. In SaaS the provider handled all of these."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A hybrid design connects on-premises Active Directory to a cloud identity provider through synchronization and federation. Which security consideration is MOST critical?",c:["Protecting the sync server and federation trust, since compromising it affects both environments","Ensuring cloud storage uses a high-performance tier so authentication requests complete quickly","Confirming the cloud provider's data center holds environmental and physical security certifications","Standardizing endpoint disk encryption so devices remain protected if they are lost"],a:0,e:["Correct. Identity is the bridge between environments, so a compromised sync account or token-signing certificate yields access to both on-premises and cloud.","Incorrect. Performance tiers affect latency, not security exposure. Authentication speed would matter for user experience planning.","Incorrect. Provider certifications matter in vendor assessment but do not address the exposure created by the identity link between environments.","Incorrect. Disk encryption protects lost endpoints but does not mitigate compromise of identity sync or federation. It addresses data at rest on devices."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"What is the MAIN security trade-off of consolidating workloads onto a virtualized environment instead of dedicated physical servers?",c:["Virtual machines cannot be encrypted, so stored data is exposed on shared disks","Virtual machines cannot be backed up while running, which increases recovery time","Many workloads depend on one hypervisor layer, so its compromise has a large blast radius","Virtual machines cannot sit behind host or network firewalls, so traffic cannot be filtered"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. VMs support disk and datastore encryption. Encryption support is not a limitation of virtualization.","Incorrect. Snapshots and agent-based backups of live VMs are common. Backup complexity is not the defining trade-off.","Correct. Consolidation concentrates risk: a hypervisor flaw or escape can expose many guests at once, unlike isolated physical servers.","Incorrect. VMs can use virtual firewalls, host firewalls and micro-segmentation. Filtering is available in virtual environments."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"An embedded controller in a vehicle cannot be patched over the air and has a known vulnerability. Which architectural control is MOST appropriate?",c:["Install host-based antivirus on the controller to block known exploits and malware signatures","Segment the vehicle network so a gateway ECU limits what traffic can reach the controller","Schedule a weekly power cycle so any malicious code in memory is cleared","Require longer passwords on the diagnostic port so unauthorized users cannot connect"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Embedded controllers lack resources and OS support for AV agents. AV fits general-purpose endpoints.","Correct. A gateway isolates safety-critical controllers from infotainment and external interfaces, compensating for the lack of patching.","Incorrect. Rebooting does not remove a vulnerability, and persistent exploits survive restarts. It is a stability tactic, not a security control.","Incorrect. Password strength does not address a known software flaw reachable over the network. Authentication hardening applies to the diagnostic interface itself."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A company is weighing ease of deployment and risk transference in selecting a SaaS application for HR data. Which statement is MOST accurate?",c:["SaaS transfers the risk to the provider, so the company has no residual obligations for the data","SaaS is typically slower to deploy than on-premises software because of extensive customization","SaaS removes regulatory obligations because the provider handles processing on the company's behalf","SaaS deploys quickly and shifts infrastructure risk, but the company still owns data governance"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Responsibility for the data and compliance stays with the customer. Only some operational risk is shifted contractually.","Incorrect. SaaS is usually faster to deploy because the provider has already built and hosts the platform.","Incorrect. Regulations such as privacy laws still bind the data owner when a processor is used. Contracts allocate duties but do not erase them.","Correct. Infrastructure risk transfers to the provider, while accountability for classification, access and compliance remains with the customer."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A monolithic retail application must handle large traffic spikes during a holiday sale. Which change BEST improves scalability and also contains failures?",c:["Refactor into services behind load balancers with autoscaling","Replace the server with a single larger instance with more CPU and memory","Move the application to an air-gapped network segment to limit exposure","Place a caching layer in front of the application and extend session timeouts"],a:0,e:["Correct. Horizontal scaling spreads load, and separated services limit how far a failure spreads.","Incorrect. Vertical scaling has a ceiling and remains one failure point. It could suffice for a modest, predictable increase in load.","Incorrect. An air gap would cut off customers. It fits isolated systems, not public sales platforms.","Incorrect. Caching helps read performance but does not scale write-heavy transactions or isolate failures. It complements scaling rather than replaces it."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which of the following is a security consideration SPECIFIC to an on-premises architecture compared with a public cloud deployment?",c:["The provider is responsible for patching the underlying hypervisor and hosts","Hardware is shared across multiple tenants, so side-channel and noisy-neighbor risks must be considered","The organization is responsible for physical security, power, cooling and hardware refresh","Elastic capacity can be added within minutes through a self-service API call"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This is characteristic of cloud models. On-premises, the organization patches its own hosts.","Incorrect. Multi-tenancy is typical of public cloud. A private on-premises environment generally has only the organization's own workloads.","Correct. On-premises, the organization owns every layer, including facility controls and hardware replacement.","Incorrect. Rapid elasticity is a cloud benefit. On-premises capacity requires procurement and installation."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"A team deploys containers pulled directly from public registries and runs them in production without any scanning. What is the MOST likely risk?",c:["Increased DNS resolution latency from repeated image pulls over the network","Inheriting known vulnerabilities or malicious code embedded in the base image","Exhaustion of the host's file descriptors on each node as many images are cached locally","Loss of image layers when the registry rotates its storage encryption keys"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Pulling images can add network load, but latency is not a security risk. It is a performance topic.","Correct. Unvetted images can carry outdated packages, backdoors or cryptominers. Scanning and trusted registries mitigate this.","Incorrect. Resource exhaustion is possible but is an operational issue. It is not the risk arising from unvetted image contents.","Incorrect. Registry key rotation does not delete layers and has nothing to do with the origin of the image. It concerns registry administration."]},
{o:"3.1",q:"Which consideration BEST explains why an organization might keep a particular workload on-premises rather than in the cloud?",c:["Cloud platforms are less secure than any on-premises facility","On-premises infrastructure costs less over the full lifecycle for most workloads","Cloud services are unable to encrypt data at rest or in transit","Regulatory, latency or control requirements that a provider cannot meet"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Cloud security can exceed many on-premises environments. A blanket claim like this is unsupported.","Incorrect. On-premises often costs more when staffing, hardware refresh and facilities are counted. Cost depends on the workload.","Incorrect. Cloud providers offer strong encryption and key management. Lack of encryption is not a real limitation.","Correct. Specific legal, performance or customization demands can justify keeping certain workloads on-premises."]},

// ---------- 3.2 (28) ----------
{o:"3.2",q:"A company deploys an inline IPS in front of its payment gateway. Leadership states that payment processing must stay up even if the IPS appliance itself fails. Which configuration, and what trade-off does it carry?",c:["Fail-closed, accepting that all payment traffic stops until the IPS is restored","Passive TAP deployment, accepting that malicious traffic is detected but never blocked","Fail-closed with an active/passive HA pair, accepting a brief outage during failover","Fail-open, accepting that traffic passes uninspected while the IPS is down"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Fail-closed blocks traffic when the device fails, which preserves security but violates the stated uptime requirement. It fits environments where confidentiality outranks availability.","Incorrect. A TAP removes the failure point but permanently turns the sensor into a detector. The requirement is for an inline device that still prevents attacks while healthy.","Incorrect. HA reduces the chance of outage, but a fail-closed design still halts traffic when both units fail. It would be right where no uninspected traffic may ever pass.","Correct. Fail-open keeps traffic flowing when the device fails, preserving availability at the cost of temporary loss of inspection."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An analyst needs a full copy of traffic on a critical link for forensic review. The copying method must not add a point of failure or alter the traffic. Which is BEST?",c:["An inline IPS configured in monitor-only mode","A passive network TAP feeding the analysis tool","A forward proxy that logs each session before relaying it","A load balancer with a mirroring policy to the monitoring port"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Inline devices still sit in the data path and can fail or add latency even in monitor mode. A TAP avoids that placement.","Correct. A TAP copies traffic without being in the path, and the link keeps working if the tool fails.","Incorrect. A proxy terminates and re-originates connections, so it alters traffic and becomes a failure point. It suits web filtering and caching.","Incorrect. A load balancer is inline and modifies flow distribution. It is meant for distributing requests across servers."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Users are allowed to browse Dropbox but must be blocked from uploading files to it. All Dropbox traffic uses TCP 443. Which control can enforce this?",c:["A next-generation firewall with application identification and TLS decryption","A stateful firewall with rules keyed on source, destination, port and connection state","A router ACL restricting TCP 443 to the Dropbox address ranges","A web application firewall placed in front of the corporate website"],a:0,e:["Correct. Distinguishing upload from download inside HTTPS requires layer 7 awareness, and decryption to see the application action.","Incorrect. Stateful inspection sees only addresses, ports and session state, and all Dropbox traffic is on 443. It fits basic perimeter filtering.","Incorrect. An ACL can permit or deny a whole destination but cannot separate uploads from browsing. It suits coarse network-level filtering.","Incorrect. A WAF protects inbound traffic to the company's own web applications, not outbound user activity to a third-party service."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A reverse proxy placed in front of web servers terminates TLS. Which security capability does this MOST directly enable?",c:["Encrypting database tables on the servers so stored records are protected","Filtering the outbound browsing of internal employees against a category list","Inspecting decrypted requests with a WAF and managing certificates in one place","Authenticating administrators to servers through one-time credentials and session logging"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Encryption at rest is a storage control, separate from proxy TLS termination. Transparent database encryption would provide it.","Incorrect. Outbound user filtering is the function of a forward proxy or secure web gateway, not a reverse proxy.","Correct. After termination, plaintext HTTP is available for inspection before reaching the servers, and certificates are managed centrally.","Incorrect. Recorded admin sessions are provided by a jump host or PAM solution. A reverse proxy is for client-facing traffic."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Where should a web application firewall be placed relative to a load balancer and the web servers it protects?",c:["Behind the database tier so it can analyze query responses and stored procedures","In front of the web servers, at or before the load balancer, to inspect HTTP requests","On the internal DNS server so it can inspect names requested by clients","On the endpoints of the administrators who manage the servers and review their logs"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A WAF must see HTTP requests before the application processes them. The database tier does not receive HTTP.","Correct. Placing it ahead of the application lets it block injection and XSS attempts before they reach the servers.","Incorrect. DNS servers handle name resolution, and DNS filtering is a separate control. A WAF inspects HTTP application traffic.","Incorrect. Endpoint protection protects administrators' workstations. A WAF protects the application from client requests."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants administrators to reach production servers only through one audited system that enforces MFA and records every session. What should it deploy?",c:["A hardened jump server (bastion) integrated with privileged access management","A forward proxy configured to log administrator web requests","A layer 4 load balancer pooling the administrative endpoints of production servers","A network access control server enforcing posture checks at the access switch"],a:0,e:["Correct. A jump server centralizes and logs administrative access, and PAM adds MFA and session recording.","Incorrect. A forward proxy mediates user web traffic outbound. It does not provide an administrative gateway to servers.","Incorrect. A load balancer spreads requests across hosts for availability, with no MFA or session recording. It does not control who administers servers.","Incorrect. NAC decides whether an endpoint may join the network. It does not funnel or record admin sessions to servers."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"In an 802.1X deployment, a switch port remains unauthorized until a RADIUS server validates the EAP-TLS certificate presented by a laptop. What role does the laptop play?",c:["Authenticator, the switch or wireless access point that enforces port state","Authentication server, the system that validates credentials","Supplicant, the endpoint presenting credentials to request access","Certificate authority, the issuer of the laptop's certificate"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The authenticator is the switch (or wireless access point) that enforces port state. The laptop is the party requesting access.","Incorrect. The authentication server is the RADIUS server that validates credentials. The laptop only presents them.","Correct. The endpoint requesting network access and presenting credentials is the supplicant.","Incorrect. The CA issues and signs certificates but does not take part in the port-based exchange. The CA matters for validating the chain."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which wireless authentication method requires certificates on BOTH the client and the authentication server, and therefore offers the strongest mutual authentication?",c:["PEAP with MSCHAPv2, which tunnels a username and password inside a server-authenticated session","EAP-FAST with PAC provisioning, which uses a shared secret for the tunnel","EAP-TTLS with a legacy inner method, which uses a server certificate and an inner password protocol","EAP-TLS, which uses X.509 certificates on both client and server"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. PEAP authenticates the server with a certificate and the user with a password. It is common when client certificates are impractical.","Incorrect. EAP-FAST uses protected access credentials instead of client certificates. It was designed to avoid PKI.","Incorrect. TTLS needs only a server certificate, with the client authenticating inside the tunnel. It fits environments without client PKI.","Correct. Both sides present certificates, giving mutual authentication with no passwords to phish."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A site-to-site IPSec VPN must provide confidentiality and integrity for the entire original packet, hiding the internal source and destination addresses. Which configuration meets this?",c:["AH in tunnel mode, which authenticates the new outer packet and its payload","ESP in tunnel mode, which encrypts and encapsulates the original packet","ESP in transport mode, which encrypts only the payload of the original packet","AH in transport mode, which authenticates the payload and parts of the header"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. AH provides integrity and authentication but no encryption, so internal addresses remain visible to eavesdroppers.","Correct. ESP provides confidentiality and integrity, and tunnel mode wraps the original IP header in a new one.","Incorrect. Transport mode leaves the original IP header in the clear, exposing internal addresses. It suits host-to-host protection.","Incorrect. AH offers no confidentiality, and transport mode keeps the original header. It fits only where integrity matters and encryption is not wanted."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"In rare cases an organization selects AH instead of ESP for an IPSec link. Which requirement would justify that choice?",c:["Integrity and origin authentication are needed, but encryption is unnecessary or restricted","Strong payload encryption is required, with the fastest possible hardware offload","The tunnel must traverse a NAT device that rewrites source addresses on the path","Confidentiality of the traffic matters, and a smaller header is preferred to reduce overhead per packet"],a:0,e:["Correct. AH authenticates the packet and parts of its IP header without providing confidentiality, which may be useful where encryption is legally restricted.","Incorrect. AH has no encryption. ESP supplies confidentiality and is also widely supported by hardware.","Incorrect. AH covers header fields that NAT changes, so it breaks across NAT. ESP with NAT-T handles that case.","Incorrect. AH offers no confidentiality, so confidentiality requirements rule it out. ESP is the protocol to use."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company with a mostly remote workforce adopts SASE. Which capability MOST distinguishes SASE from a traditional VPN back to headquarters?",c:["It encrypts traffic between the user and headquarters using IPSec tunnels","It relies on on-premises appliances to inspect all user traffic at the data center","It delivers SWG, CASB, ZTNA and firewall functions from cloud points of presence near users","It authenticates users once per day and trusts the device for the rest of the session without rechecking posture"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Traditional VPNs also encrypt traffic and may use IPSec. Encryption does not distinguish SASE.","Incorrect. Backhauling to HQ appliances is the traditional model that SASE replaces. SASE inspects at cloud points of presence.","Correct. SASE converges networking and security as a cloud service close to the user, rather than routing everything through HQ.","Incorrect. SASE aligns with continuous, identity-driven verification. Long-lived trust is the legacy VPN approach."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which layer 2 attack is MOST directly mitigated by limiting the number of MAC addresses allowed per switch port?",c:["ARP poisoning that maps the attacker's MAC to a gateway address","Rogue DHCP servers offering malicious gateway and DNS settings to clients","VLAN hopping through double 802.1Q tagging on the native VLAN of a trunk port","MAC flooding that overflows the CAM table so the switch floods frames"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. ARP poisoning uses the attacker's own MAC, which port security allows. Dynamic ARP Inspection mitigates this.","Incorrect. A rogue DHCP server requires DHCP snooping with trusted ports to stop it. Port security does not examine DHCP messages.","Incorrect. Double tagging is addressed by changing the native VLAN and disabling unused trunks. MAC limits do not affect it.","Correct. Limiting learned MACs per port prevents a single port from exhausting the CAM table and forcing the switch to behave like a hub."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A network engineer enables DHCP snooping on access switches and marks only uplink ports as trusted. Which attack does this primarily prevent?",c:["A rogue DHCP server handing out a malicious default gateway and DNS settings","MAC flooding that exhausts the CAM table of the access switch and floods frames","VLAN double tagging used to reach another VLAN through the native VLAN","Spanning tree root hijacking through crafted BPDUs from an access port"],a:0,e:["Correct. Only trusted ports may send DHCP offers, so a rogue server on an access port is blocked.","Incorrect. Port security limits MAC addresses per port to stop flooding. DHCP snooping examines DHCP messages.","Incorrect. Double tagging is prevented through native VLAN changes and trunk hardening, not DHCP controls.","Incorrect. BPDU guard and root guard protect STP. DHCP snooping has no effect on spanning tree."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A firewall rule base reads: (1) Deny host 10.1.1.50 to any; (2) Allow 10.1.1.0/24 to the web server on TCP 443; (3) Deny all. Host 10.1.1.50 attempts to reach the web server on TCP 443. What happens?",c:["It is permitted, because rule 2 covers the whole /24 and allows port 443","It is dropped, because rule 1 is the first matching rule","It is permitted, because HTTPS traffic is implicitly allowed unless explicitly denied","It is dropped, because the final deny-all overrides earlier permit rules"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Rule 2 does cover the host, but rules are processed top-down and rule 1 already matched. If rule 2 were listed first, this host would be permitted.","Correct. Firewalls evaluate rules in order and apply the first match, so the host-specific deny takes effect.","Incorrect. Firewalls do not implicitly allow ports, and rule 1 explicitly denies this host anyway. Default-allow configurations are rare and risky.","Incorrect. The outcome is a drop, but the reasoning is wrong. A final deny only catches traffic not matched by earlier rules."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants to stop unknown devices from joining its wired network and to place non-compliant managed devices into a remediation VLAN. Which solution is MOST appropriate?",c:["An IDS sensor with current signature updates on the access-layer switches","A layer 7 load balancer with health checks and session persistence for client devices","A reverse proxy with client certificate checks for the internal web applications","Network access control with 802.1X authentication and posture assessment"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An IDS only detects and alerts. It cannot authenticate devices or reassign ports.","Incorrect. Load balancers distribute server traffic and check server health, not client posture.","Incorrect. A reverse proxy controls access to web applications. It does not govern network admission.","Correct. NAC authenticates endpoints, evaluates compliance and assigns them to production or quarantine VLANs."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A large enterprise with very high throughput and strict uptime needs is evaluating a single UTM appliance instead of separate specialized devices. What is the main drawback?",c:["Signature updates must be applied offline, so protection lags behind dedicated point products","The appliance works only at layer 3 and layer 4, so it cannot apply application-aware or content-based policy","Every function shares one platform, so enabling all features cuts throughput and adds a single point of failure","Web filtering and IPS functions require an agent installed on every protected endpoint and server to function"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. UTMs receive regular subscription updates. Offline updating is typical of isolated or air-gapped devices.","Incorrect. That is a limitation of packet-filter devices. UTMs include application and content inspection.","Correct. Consolidation is convenient but concentrates risk and processing load, which is a problem at enterprise scale.","Incorrect. UTMs filter inline without endpoint agents. Agents are used by cloud-based secure web gateway clients."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A network IDS sensor connected to a SPAN port misses packets during traffic spikes. What is the MOST likely explanation?",c:["The switch encrypts mirrored frames, so the sensor discards them as unreadable when the traffic rate rises","SPAN is best-effort and drops mirrored frames when monitored traffic exceeds the mirror port capacity","The sensor is in non-promiscuous mode and only sees traffic addressed to its own interface","The IPS fail-open bypass engaged, so traffic skips the sensor under load"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. SPAN does not encrypt frames. Encrypted payloads would affect content inspection, not packet delivery.","Correct. Mirroring has lower priority than forwarding and is oversubscribed during bursts. A TAP gives a more reliable copy.","Incorrect. That would cause packet loss at all times, not only during spikes. Promiscuous mode is required for monitoring.","Incorrect. Fail-open applies to inline devices. A sensor on a SPAN port is passive and not in the data path."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company needs to replace SNMPv2c for managing network devices because community strings travel in cleartext. Which option is BEST?",c:["SNMPv3 using authPriv with per-user credentials","SNMPv1 with a rotated community string and an ACL on the management VLAN","Telnet with a TACACS+ login for each device","TFTP with configuration backups stored on a file server"],a:0,e:["Correct. SNMPv3 authPriv provides authentication and encryption, replacing cleartext community strings.","Incorrect. SNMPv1 still sends community strings in cleartext, so rotation and ACLs only reduce exposure. It lacks encryption.","Incorrect. Telnet is cleartext, and TACACS+ protects only the AAA exchange. SSH would be the secure CLI option.","Incorrect. TFTP is unauthenticated and unencrypted, used for file transfer rather than device management."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants remote workers' internet traffic to reach the internet directly instead of through HQ, while still enforcing web policy. Which VPN setting and companion control fit?",c:["Full tunneling combined with a headquarters web proxy and SSL inspection","Split tunneling combined with unrestricted direct internet access from the laptop","Full tunneling combined with client certificates on every managed laptop and mobile device","Split tunneling combined with a cloud secure web gateway or endpoint filtering"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Full tunneling backhauls everything through HQ, which is what the company wants to avoid. It suits strict inspection of all traffic.","Incorrect. Split tunneling without controls leaves direct traffic unfiltered. Policy enforcement is a stated requirement.","Incorrect. Certificates strengthen authentication but still send all traffic via HQ. They do not enable direct routing.","Correct. Internet-bound traffic goes direct, while cloud filtering keeps policy enforcement."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"What is the primary security benefit of placing public servers in a screened subnet protected by two firewalls from different vendors?",c:["It removes the need to patch the public servers because attacks are filtered twice","It speeds up web traffic because each firewall handles half of the sessions","Vendor diversity means one firewall flaw does not expose the internal network","It stops distributed denial-of-service attacks from reaching the servers"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Layered firewalls reduce exposure but do not remove server vulnerabilities. Patching is still required.","Incorrect. Series firewalls add inspection latency rather than sharing load. Load sharing is a clustering function.","Correct. Different vendors reduce common-mode vulnerabilities, and the second firewall still protects the internal network.","Incorrect. Firewalls cannot absorb volumetric DDoS. Upstream scrubbing or a CDN addresses that."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"An IDS repeatedly alerts on IIS exploit attempts, but the environment contains only Linux servers running Apache. How should these alerts be classified, and what reduces them?",c:["False negatives; deploy more sensors to widen coverage of the server subnets","False positives from irrelevant signatures; tune rules to the asset inventory","True positives; escalate to incident response for immediate investigation and containment","True negatives; disable alerting for the affected signature class on all sensors"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A false negative is a missed attack. These are alerts that fired when they should not have.","Correct. The signatures target software that does not exist here, so tuning removes noise.","Incorrect. A true positive is a real, applicable attack. These alerts do not apply to the environment.","Incorrect. A true negative is correct silence. Here the sensor produces alerts, so the classification and fix are wrong."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which protocol is BEST for centrally authenticating network administrators, authorizing individual commands and encrypting the entire payload of the exchange?",c:["TACACS+, which separates authentication, authorization and accounting and encrypts the payload","RADIUS, which combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password field","LDAPS, which provides directory lookups over TLS for user attributes","Kerberos, which issues time-limited tickets for single sign-on inside a domain"],a:0,e:["Correct. TACACS+ supports per-command authorization and encrypts the whole body of the packet.","Incorrect. RADIUS encrypts only the password and lacks granular per-command authorization. It is typically used for network access such as 802.1X and VPNs.","Incorrect. LDAPS is a directory access protocol. It stores identities but does not authorize device commands.","Incorrect. Kerberos provides authentication for domain resources, not command authorization on network devices."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"Which attack would a layer 7 firewall MOST likely stop that a layer 4 firewall cannot?",c:["A port scan across the server subnet to map open TCP and UDP ports","A SYN flood against a web server aimed at exhausting its connection table","IP address spoofing from an external network using forged internal source addresses","Malicious commands inside an allowed protocol, such as SQL injection over HTTPS"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Layer 4 devices see ports and can detect or block scans.","Incorrect. Layer 4 firewalls can track connection state and apply SYN protection.","Incorrect. Anti-spoofing checks are applied at layer 3 and 4 using ingress filtering.","Correct. Layer 7 inspection reads the application payload, where the attack lives, while layer 4 sees only an allowed port."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company wants to ensure that an attacker who compromises a DMZ web server cannot start connections into the internal network. Which rule set is BEST?",c:["Permit DMZ to internal on any port with full logging enabled for later review","Permit DMZ to the internal subnet on TCP 3389 so the server team can administer remotely","Permit only specific internal-to-DMZ flows and deny DMZ-initiated connections inward","Permit DMZ to internal only during business hours and from approved server addresses"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Logging does not block anything, and any-port access lets the attacker pivot. Logging is useful for detection.","Incorrect. Opening RDP from DMZ to internal gives the attacker a direct lateral-movement path.","Correct. Servers in the DMZ should receive connections but not initiate them inward, containing a compromise.","Incorrect. A time restriction does not stop a compromised host from initiating inward connections during permitted hours."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A load balancer performs SSL offloading for a pool of web servers. Which security consideration is MOST relevant?",c:["Data stored on the web servers is left unencrypted because the load balancer handles encryption","Traffic from the load balancer to back-end servers may be cleartext unless re-encrypted","The load balancer stops SQL injection on its own because it sees decrypted requests","The web servers no longer need valid certificates because the load balancer is the only endpoint"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Offloading concerns TLS in transit and has no effect on data at rest, which depends on storage encryption.","Correct. After termination, internal traffic is unencrypted by default, so end-to-end TLS or re-encryption may be needed.","Incorrect. Visibility is not inspection. A WAF or similar function must analyze the requests.","Incorrect. Certificates are still needed, on the load balancer and often on back ends for re-encryption."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A remote user connects over a TLS-based solution that verifies device posture and then grants access to specific applications only, not the whole network. Which model does this represent?",c:["Zero Trust Network Access, granting per-application access by identity and device posture","A traditional full-tunnel remote access VPN, granting access to the internal network once connected","A site-to-site IPSec tunnel, linking two office networks permanently and carrying all traffic between them","A screened subnet, hosting public services behind reverse proxies and firewalls"],a:0,e:["Correct. Per-application access based on identity and device posture is the core of ZTNA.","Incorrect. A traditional VPN typically grants network-level access once connected. Per-application scoping is absent.","Incorrect. Site-to-site VPNs connect networks, not individual users or devices with posture checks.","Incorrect. A screened subnet hosts public services. It does not verify device posture or scope access per user."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A company adds a new SaaS integration that uses long-lived API keys to read data from its CRM. Which statement BEST describes the effect on the attack surface?",c:["It shrinks, because fewer manual exports and file transfers are needed","It stays the same, because API traffic is encrypted in transit with TLS 1.2 or higher","It applies only to physical security, because APIs run in the cloud provider's data centers","It grows, because the integration adds a trusted path and credentials to target"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Automation may reduce manual handling, but it does not reduce attack surface. Each integration adds exposure.","Incorrect. TLS protects data in transit but does not remove the new endpoint and credentials that attackers can target.","Incorrect. API exposure is a logical attack surface. Physical security is unrelated to key theft or abuse.","Correct. Every new integration adds endpoints and secrets, which must be protected, scoped and monitored."]},
{o:"3.2",q:"A switch is configured with 'switchport mode access' on user ports and 'switchport nonegotiate'. Which attack is this configuration preventing?",c:["MAC flooding that overflows the CAM table","ARP poisoning against the default gateway","VLAN hopping through switch spoofing with DTP","Spanning tree manipulation to become the root bridge"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Limiting MAC addresses with port security stops flooding. Disabling trunk negotiation has no effect.","Incorrect. Dynamic ARP Inspection addresses ARP spoofing, not DTP settings.","Correct. Forcing access mode and disabling DTP stops an attacker from negotiating a trunk and reaching other VLANs.","Incorrect. BPDU guard and root guard defend against STP manipulation. Trunk negotiation is unrelated."]},

// ---------- 3.3 (18) ----------
{o:"3.3",q:"A subscription-billing application must be able to charge stored cards on a recurring basis, while the company wants to keep most of its systems out of PCI DSS scope. Which technique is BEST?",c:["Masking all but the last four digits in the database and in application logs","Tokenization, with real card numbers held only in a secured token vault","Salted hashing of each card number before it is stored in the billing database","Field-level encryption of card numbers with a key that is copied to every billing server"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Masking changes how a value is displayed or stored in part, but the full number must still exist somewhere to charge the card. It suits display and test data.","Correct. Systems store non-sensitive tokens that can still trigger a charge through the vault or processor, so most systems fall out of PCI scope.","Incorrect. A hash is one-way, so the card cannot be charged from it. Hashing suits password verification rather than reusable payment credentials.","Incorrect. Encrypted card data stays in scope, and distributing the key to every server widens the scope. Encryption is acceptable only with tight key separation."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A research team will analyze patient records for population trends. It must be impossible to identify individuals, and the data will never need to be linked back to a patient. Which approach is MOST appropriate?",c:["Tokenization with the token-to-value mapping stored in a secured vault","Encryption of the dataset using a symmetric key held by the research team","Pseudonymization with a lookup table kept by the data custodian","Anonymization that irreversibly generalizes or removes identifiers"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A vault mapping lets authorized parties re-identify patients. It suits cases where the original value must be recoverable.","Incorrect. Anyone with the key can decrypt and identify patients. Encryption protects confidentiality but is reversible by design.","Incorrect. Pseudonymized data can be re-linked using the table. It fits studies that may need follow-up with individual patients.","Correct. Irreversible de-identification meets a requirement that re-identification never be possible, while keeping the data useful for statistics."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which statement BEST distinguishes data masking from tokenization?",c:["Masking hides or alters values, often irreversibly, while tokenization substitutes surrogates that map back via a vault","Masking replaces data with random values that can be reversed with a key, while tokenization produces one-way hashes","Masking requires a central vault to track every value, while tokenization permanently removes identifiers from the data set","Masking derives the replacement mathematically from the original value, while tokenization encrypts the whole data store"],a:0,e:["Correct. They have different mechanisms: masking is for hiding data in views or test sets, while tokens preserve a controlled path back to the real value.","Incorrect. This reverses the concepts. Tokens are surrogates that a vault can map back, and masking is generally not keyed reversal.","Incorrect. A vault is characteristic of tokenization, and tokenization is reversible through it. Permanent removal describes anonymization.","Incorrect. Tokens have no mathematical relationship to the original, and tokenization does not encrypt a data store. Format-preserving encryption is the mathematical approach."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"An insurer processes claims in a public cloud and wants the data to remain protected even while computations run, so the provider's administrators cannot see plaintext in memory. Which approach fits?",c:["TLS 1.3 between the application tiers and the database with mutual authentication","Full-disk encryption on the virtual machine storage volumes and snapshots","Confidential computing using enclaves or homomorphic encryption on claim data","Tokenization of the claim fields before they are stored in the claims database"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. TLS protects data in transit. Data is decrypted when it is processed in memory.","Incorrect. FDE protects data at rest. Contents are exposed in plaintext while the workload runs.","Correct. These protect data in use by keeping it encrypted or isolated in a hardware enclave while it is processed.","Incorrect. Tokenization protects stored values but the processing step still works with real data or must detokenize it. It does not protect memory contents."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A multinational must keep EU customer data within the EU, yet its support team is based in the US. Which approach BEST balances support needs and data sovereignty?",c:["Replicate the full customer database to a US region and apply role-based access for the support team","Keep data in EU regions and give US staff only masked or approved subsets under legal safeguards","Export weekly reports containing customer details to US staff over an encrypted email channel","Allow US employees to use their own devices to connect and download records for offline review"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Replicating the full data set to the US moves it outside the EU, regardless of access roles. It would be appropriate only with a lawful transfer mechanism and a non-sovereignty-bound data set.","Correct. Location restrictions, minimization and contractual safeguards together meet both sovereignty and support requirements.","Incorrect. Encryption protects transit but the data still leaves the EU in an uncontrolled way. Sovereignty obligations are not met.","Incorrect. Downloads to personal devices move EU data abroad and weaken control. Remote access should be through controlled, monitored sessions."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which classification label would MOST likely apply to unreleased merger negotiation documents at a public company?",c:["Public, because the transaction will eventually be announced","Internal, because only employees are involved in the negotiations","Confidential, because the information is intended only for the legal team","Restricted, because leaks could cause severe financial and legal harm"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Public data is approved for release. The documents are not yet released and premature disclosure would cause serious harm.","Incorrect. Internal data is for general employee use. Merger material is limited to a small group and warrants a higher level.","Incorrect. Confidential is a high tier but the highest sensitivity is reserved for material whose disclosure would be most damaging, such as insider information about a pending deal.","Correct. Pending M&A information is typically at the highest sensitivity tier, because leaks can affect markets and create regulatory exposure."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company hashes customer Social Security numbers with SHA-256 (no salt) to use them as database keys, believing this protects them. What is the flaw?",c:["SSNs have a small keyspace, so the hashes can be brute-forced to recover the originals","SHA-256 is a reversible algorithm, so the SSNs can be decoded with the appropriate secret key","SHA-256 output is truncated, so many SSNs collide and records are overwritten","Hashing alters the field length, so the database indexes cannot use the values as keys"],a:0,e:["Correct. Low-entropy values are easy to enumerate. A keyed HMAC or tokenization would be stronger.","Incorrect. Hash functions are one-way and use no key. The risk is guessing inputs, not decoding the output.","Incorrect. SHA-256 produces a 256-bit digest and collisions are not practical. The weakness is the small input space.","Incorrect. Fixed-length digests work well as keys. Length is not a security concern here."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which data state is being protected when TLS secures the connection between a microservice and its database?",c:["Data at rest on the database server's storage volumes and snapshots","Data in use in the memory of the application process on the service host","Data in transit across the network between the two services","Data in archive form on long-term offline backup media and tape"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. At-rest protection requires disk, volume or database encryption. TLS secures only the network connection.","Incorrect. Protecting data in use requires enclaves or similar technology. TLS ends at the endpoint, where the data is decrypted.","Correct. TLS encrypts data as it moves across the network.","Incorrect. Archives are at rest, and backup encryption, not TLS, protects them."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A DLP system blocks upload of a file labeled 'Confidential' to a personal cloud storage account. The label that triggered the block originated from which process?",c:["Hashing of file contents to produce a unique fingerprint used to match known files","Data classification, in which sensitivity labels are applied to drive handling","DNS filtering policy that blocks categories of consumer storage domains at the resolver","Certificate validation performed by the secure web gateway during the upload"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A hash can detect exact copies of known files but does not assign a sensitivity label. It could support fingerprint-based DLP matching.","Correct. DLP rules rely on classification labels to decide how to handle the data.","Incorrect. DNS filtering could block the site, but it does not assign a label to the file.","Incorrect. Certificate validation checks the identity of the server, not the sensitivity of the file."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which option BEST protects proprietary source code in a Git repository from theft by an insider?",c:["Strong password complexity and 90-day password expiry for repository accounts","Full-disk encryption on developer laptops and encrypted repository backups in the cloud","Network segmentation of the repository server into a restricted subnet with ACLs","Least-privilege repository access, clone and download monitoring, and audit logging"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Password policy helps against outside guessing but does nothing to limit what an authorized insider can copy. It does not give visibility.","Incorrect. Encryption protects lost devices and media, but insiders legitimately access the decrypted code. It addresses theft of hardware.","Incorrect. Segmentation limits network exposure, but insiders with permitted access can still clone the code. It is useful alongside access controls.","Correct. Limiting access, watching for bulk activity and keeping attribution addresses the insider threat directly."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company masks production data for a test environment, but testers can still determine individuals' identities by combining ZIP code, birth date and gender. What is the issue?",c:["A tokenization failure, because the vault mapping was exposed to the testers","An encryption failure, because the test database is not protected at rest with transparent encryption","Re-identification risk from quasi-identifiers, needing generalization or suppression","A hashing failure, because the test environment reuses production salts"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. No tokens or vault were involved. The weakness is in the remaining attributes of the data.","Incorrect. Encryption at rest does not address inference from visible attributes. Testers are authorized to read the data.","Correct. Combinations of ordinary attributes can uniquely identify people, so they must be generalized, suppressed or perturbed.","Incorrect. Salts are not part of the scenario. Identity is inferred from visible fields."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which statement about data sovereignty is MOST accurate?",c:["Data is subject to the laws of the country where it is collected or stored, which can conflict","Data sovereignty applies mainly to government data, and most private-sector data is exempt","Data sovereignty obligations end once data is encrypted at rest with a customer-held key","Data sovereignty compliance is handled by the cloud provider on behalf of the customer under its standard terms"],a:0,e:["Correct. Jurisdiction follows the data, and multinational organizations face overlapping requirements.","Incorrect. Private data about individuals and organizations is covered by many national laws. The rules are not limited to government records.","Incorrect. Encryption is a protective measure but does not change the jurisdiction in which the data resides. Laws may still apply.","Incorrect. The customer remains accountable for where its data is stored and processed. Providers offer region controls that the customer must configure."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company wants to ensure a sensitive document cannot be opened by anyone outside the organization, even after it is emailed to an external address. Which technology provides this?",c:["Data masking that obscures sensitive fields before the file is sent externally","Information rights management with restrictions that travel with the file","File hashing that records the digest of the document before transmission","Email transport encryption using opportunistic TLS between mail servers"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Masking alters the content shown but does not enforce who can open the file. It protects fields in views and test sets.","Correct. IRM or DRM enforces access and usage rights through encryption bound to identity, wherever the file goes.","Incorrect. A hash verifies integrity only. It does not prevent opening the file.","Incorrect. TLS protects the message in transit but the recipient can open the file once delivered. It does not control the document afterward."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"In data classification, which statement BEST describes the difference between 'critical data' and 'sensitive data'?",c:["Critical data is always publicly releasable, while sensitive data is always private","The terms are interchangeable, and both refer to data that must be encrypted","Sensitive data supports business operations, while critical data would cause harm if disclosed","Critical data is essential to operations, while sensitive data would cause harm if disclosed"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Critical data may be private as well. The categories are defined by different concerns, not by public release.","Incorrect. Encryption needs depend on sensitivity and regulation. Criticality concerns operational importance.","Incorrect. This swaps the definitions. Critical relates to operational importance and sensitive to harm from disclosure.","Correct. A public price list can be critical to a store without being sensitive. Medical records are sensitive and may not be critical to daily operations."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"An organization encrypts a customer database but stores the encryption keys in a table in the same database. What is the MAIN weakness?",c:["Query performance falls because each row must be decrypted at read time","Backup size increases because encrypted data does not compress well","An attacker who obtains the database gets both the ciphertext and the keys","Replication lag increases because keys must be synchronized across all cluster nodes"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Performance overhead is a trade-off of encryption, not the security flaw here.","Incorrect. Storage growth is an operational concern. It is unrelated to key placement.","Correct. Keys must be separated from data, such as in a KMS or HSM.","Incorrect. Replication delay is a performance issue and not the security problem."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which method BEST restricts access to a sensitive dataset so that it can be opened only when the user is physically inside corporate offices?",c:["Geolocation- or geofence-based conditional access policy","Dynamic data masking applied to the sensitive columns","Salted hashing of the dataset's identifiers before storage","Data deduplication on the storage array holding the dataset"],a:0,e:["Correct. Location-aware policies allow or deny access depending on where the user or network is located.","Incorrect. Masking hides values but does not take location into account. It would serve to limit what data is displayed to certain roles.","Incorrect. Hashing protects values but has no effect on where access originates.","Incorrect. Deduplication saves storage space and is not an access control."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"A company publishes its annual report after redacting customer names. Which data classification applies to the PUBLISHED report?",c:["Restricted, because the report was derived from sensitive records","Confidential, because the report originated from internal records","Regulated, because the underlying data included customer information","Public, because it has been approved for external release"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Restricted is for data whose disclosure is highly damaging. The published version has been approved for release.","Incorrect. Once the redacted report is intentionally released, it no longer requires confidentiality. Confidential applies to the unredacted source.","Incorrect. Regulated status applies to data such as personal or financial records. The redacted report no longer contains that data.","Correct. Information that is intentionally released has a public classification."]},
{o:"3.3",q:"Which protection is MOST appropriate for data in use on an endpoint that processes highly classified material?",c:["TLS 1.3 for every connection the endpoint makes to internal services","A secure enclave or trusted execution environment on the endpoint","Nightly encrypted backups of the endpoint sent to an offsite location","Dynamic data masking of sensitive fields shown in reports and dashboards"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. TLS protects data in transit. Data being processed in memory is not covered.","Correct. Enclaves protect memory during processing, addressing the in-use state.","Incorrect. Backups address availability and recovery of data at rest, not protection of data being processed.","Incorrect. Masking limits display of values to users but does not protect process memory. It suits reports and test data."]},

// ---------- 3.4 (20) ----------
{o:"3.4",q:"Full backups run Sunday and incrementals run Monday through Saturday nights. A server fails Friday afternoon, and the team wants to restore to Thursday night. How many backup sets are required?",c:["Two sets: the Sunday full and the Thursday night incremental backup","One set: the Thursday incremental, which holds all changes since Sunday","Five sets: the Sunday full and the Monday to Thursday incrementals","Six sets: the Sunday full plus Monday through Friday incrementals"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This is how a differential works, since each differential contains all changes since the full. Incrementals contain only changes since the previous backup.","Incorrect. An incremental holds only one day of changes and cannot rebuild the system without the full and earlier incrementals.","Correct. Incrementals chain from the last backup, so the full and every incremental through Thursday are needed.","Incorrect. The Friday incremental has not run yet because the failure occurred Friday afternoon, and the target is Thursday night."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A trading platform requires an RPO of 5 minutes and an RTO of 1 hour for its database. Which solution BEST meets both?",c:["Continuous replication to a warm or hot standby with tested failover","Nightly incremental backups sent to a vaulted cloud repository with 90-day retention","Weekly full backups with daily differentials stored on a local array","Hourly snapshots stored in the same storage array as the database"],a:0,e:["Correct. Replication keeps data loss within minutes, and a standby that is ready to take over meets the one-hour recovery time.","Incorrect. Nightly backups can lose up to a day of data, far above a 5-minute RPO. Restoring from them also takes longer than an hour for large databases.","Incorrect. Even daily differentials leave many hours of potential loss. This pattern suits low-criticality systems.","Incorrect. Hourly snapshots exceed the RPO, and snapshots on the same array are lost if the array fails. They suit quick rollback of minor errors."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company builds a hot site 30 miles from its primary data center, in the same river flood plain. What is the main flaw in this design?",c:["Hot sites replicate too slowly to meet typical recovery time and recovery point objectives","Hot sites cannot hold production data, so they only hold hardware and network connectivity","Hot sites require identical hardware vendors, which limits the company's flexibility","Geographic dispersion is insufficient, so one regional disaster could hit both locations"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Hot sites are the fastest option for recovery. Speed is their strength.","Incorrect. Hot sites hold current data through replication. Hardware-only sites describe cold sites.","Incorrect. Vendor identity is not a requirement for hot sites. Diversity is an additional design principle.","Correct. Recovery sites should be far enough away and have a different hazard profile than the primary site."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which disaster recovery test gives the highest confidence in the plan but carries the greatest risk to the business?",c:["Tabletop exercise in which the team talks through a scenario without touching systems","Full interruption test that shuts down primary systems and fails over production","Walkthrough in which each owner reviews the written steps of their section","Parallel test that brings up the recovery site while production continues to run"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A tabletop poses no operational risk but yields lower confidence in technical recovery. It is useful for process and communication.","Correct. It is the most realistic test, but it can cause an outage if the recovery does not work.","Incorrect. A walkthrough validates documentation but does not exercise systems. It is low risk and low assurance.","Incorrect. Parallel testing validates recovery capability without interrupting production, so its risk is lower."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A warm site is stocked with hardware and restores data from nightly backups. The business requires an RPO of 1 hour. What is the gap?",c:["The site needs additional power capacity to support the restored workloads","The recovery time objective is too short for a warm site to meet with restored hardware","Nightly backups allow up to 24 hours of data loss, so backups must be more frequent","The site lacks network connectivity redundancy to reach the primary location"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Power capacity affects the ability to run workloads, not how much data is lost. It would be a concern if the issue were RTO.","Incorrect. The mismatch concerns data loss, and RTO is a separate measure. A warm site can usually meet an RTO of hours to days.","Correct. Backup frequency determines RPO, so an hourly RPO needs more frequent protection.","Incorrect. Connectivity redundancy affects availability of replication links, but the stated problem is the age of the restored data."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which power design BEST allows a data center to keep running through a regional outage lasting three days?",c:["A UPS sized for 30 minutes of runtime on all racks and network closets","Surge protectors on every rack and redundant PDUs fed from separate circuits and breakers","Dual utility feeds from separate substations with automatic transfer switches","A UPS for transfer, a generator with a fuel contract, and dual utility feeds"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A UPS bridges short gaps and allows graceful shutdown, but it cannot sustain operations for days.","Incorrect. Surge protection and PDUs distribute and condition power but do not generate or store it.","Incorrect. A regional outage may take down both feeds. Dual feeds help against a single line fault.","Correct. Layered power provides immediate battery coverage, long-duration generation, and logistics for refueling."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"After a ransomware incident, a company wants backups that cannot be altered or deleted for 30 days, even by an administrator whose credentials are stolen. Which feature meets this?",c:["Immutable (WORM) backup storage with a retention lock","Differential backup scheduling with a longer retention period","Synchronous mirroring of the backup volume to a second array","RAID 6 on the backup repository to survive two disk failures"],a:0,e:["Correct. Immutability prevents modification and deletion for the defined period, defeating attackers who reach the backup system.","Incorrect. A differential is a backup method and does not prevent deletion. Retention alone can be overridden by an administrator.","Incorrect. Mirroring replicates deletions and encryption to the second copy immediately.","Incorrect. RAID protects against disk failure, not malicious deletion or encryption."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Why is RAID not considered a backup?",c:["It stores data in a proprietary format that cannot be restored to other hardware","It protects against disk failure but replicates deletions, corruption and encryption by malware","It cannot be combined with encryption, which backups are required to use under most compliance regimes","It is limited to a single server and cannot protect clustered or virtualized workloads"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The format may be controller-specific, but the main issue is what RAID protects against, not portability.","Correct. RAID provides availability, not point-in-time recovery.","Incorrect. RAID can be used with encrypted volumes. Encryption is not the distinguishing factor.","Incorrect. Clusters use RAID in their nodes. The limitation is not related to the number of servers."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company runs primary and secondary sites with the same backup software from one vendor. A defect in that software corrupts the backups at both sites. Which principle would have reduced this risk?",c:["Clustering of the backup servers at each location for local failover","Capacity planning for backup storage growth across both sites","Load balancing of backup jobs across multiple backup servers at each site","Platform diversity, using different backup vendors at each site"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Clustering adds redundancy within a site, but both clusters would run the same flawed software.","Incorrect. Capacity planning prevents running out of space, not common-mode software defects.","Incorrect. Load balancing spreads work but retains the same software and its defects.","Correct. Diversity prevents one flaw from taking down both copies."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which statement BEST contrasts clustering with load balancing?",c:["Load balancing requires shared storage between nodes, while clustering uses independent storage per node","Clustering distributes requests across independent servers, while load balancing lets nodes act as one system with failover","Clustering makes nodes act as one system with failover, while load balancing spreads requests across independent servers","Clustering provides scalability only, while load balancing provides failover and session persistence only"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Load balancers often distribute to independent servers with no shared storage. Clusters frequently use shared or replicated state.","Incorrect. This reverses the definitions.","Correct. The terms overlap in practice, but their primary purposes differ.","Incorrect. Clusters are designed for failover, and load balancers can detect failed servers and remove them."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"During a tabletop exercise, the team discovers that nobody knows who can authorize a failover at 3 a.m. Which element of the plan is missing?",c:["Defined roles, decision authority and escalation contacts","Redundant servers at the alternate processing site","Higher bandwidth between the primary and recovery sites","Encryption of the backup media in transit to the recovery site"],a:0,e:["Correct. Tabletops reveal people and process gaps, such as undefined authority.","Incorrect. Hardware capacity affects whether failover can occur, not who may authorize it.","Incorrect. Bandwidth affects replication speed and is a technical performance matter.","Incorrect. Encryption protects confidentiality of backups but does not clarify decision authority."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"After restoring systems from backup following a breach, which step is MOST important before returning them to production?",c:["Delete the old logs to prevent a reinfection from log-borne malware and free up space","Verify backup integrity, close the exploited vulnerability, and monitor for reinfection","Reconnect the systems to the network immediately to meet the recovery time objective","Suspend future backups until the investigation concludes to avoid copying malware"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Logs provide the evidence needed for forensics and for confirming the attack path. Deleting them hinders recovery.","Correct. Otherwise the same weakness or attacker persistence returns with the restored data.","Incorrect. Speed matters, but reconnecting unpatched systems risks repeating the incident.","Incorrect. Suspending backups removes the safety net. Backups should continue, with integrity checks."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A continuity plan depends on employees working from home during a facility disaster. Which capacity consideration is MOST important?",c:["Spare desk, monitor and docking station stock at the alternate office location","Parking and building access badge capacity at the alternate recovery location","Print capacity and mailroom throughput for paper-based approval processes","VPN capacity and licensing sufficient for all staff at the same time"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Physical workspace supply matters for a recovery facility, not for remote work.","Incorrect. These apply to on-site recovery and are not relevant when staff work remotely.","Incorrect. Printing is not critical to remote continuity, and paper processes may be unavailable at home.","Correct. Remote access must scale to full headcount at once, or the plan fails."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which backup type captures only the data changed since the last FULL backup, growing larger each day until the next full backup?",c:["Differential backup, run nightly between weekly full backups","Incremental backup, run nightly between weekly full backups","Snapshot-based backup, taken nightly from the storage array","Journal-based backup, replayed nightly from the transaction log"],a:0,e:["Correct. Each differential includes all changes since the last full backup, so it grows until the next full.","Incorrect. An incremental contains changes since the last backup of any type, so it stays small. It requires more sets for a restore.","Incorrect. A snapshot is a point-in-time image of storage and does not describe changes relative to the last full backup.","Incorrect. Journaling logs changes for crash consistency. It is not a backup type tied to full backups."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A cloud workload runs entirely in one availability zone, and the provider suffers a zone outage. Which design would have prevented the downtime?",c:["Larger instance sizes with more memory and CPU in the same zone","More frequent snapshots stored in the same availability zone","Multi-AZ deployment with load balancing and replicated data","Reserved-instance pricing to guarantee capacity in the zone"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. More capacity does not help if the zone is unavailable.","Incorrect. Snapshots in the failed zone may be unavailable too. They help restore after data loss, not keep services running.","Correct. Spreading across zones keeps the service available when one zone fails.","Incorrect. Reserved pricing secures capacity and cost, but it does not make the zone more resilient."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which metric BEST supports capacity planning for staffing a security operations center?",c:["Count of physical access badge reads per month","Alert volume and mean time to respond trends","Number of endpoint licenses purchased for the antivirus product","Annual hardware depreciation of SOC workstations"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Badge reads reflect building traffic, not analyst workload.","Correct. Workload and response metrics show how many analysts are needed.","Incorrect. License count describes coverage but does not measure analyst effort.","Incorrect. Depreciation is a financial figure and not a measure of workload."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A company encrypts its backups but stores the only copy of the decryption key on the server that is being backed up. The server is destroyed. What happens?",c:["Backups restore normally because each backup set embeds a protected copy of the key","The cloud storage provider can decrypt the backups using its account recovery process","The operating system regenerates the same key from the restored hardware ID and TPM state","The backups cannot be decrypted; keys must be escrowed separately from the protected server"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Embedding the key in the backup would defeat encryption. Keys are normally stored separately.","Incorrect. A provider cannot decrypt customer-encrypted data without the key. Customer-managed keys remain the customer's responsibility.","Incorrect. Keys are generated randomly and are not derived from hardware identifiers.","Correct. Key management is a part of recoverability."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"Which approach BEST validates that a disaster recovery plan can meet a 4-hour RTO?",c:["A timed failover test measuring actual recovery time against the target","A document review by the continuity team to confirm the steps are complete","A vendor attestation that the recovery site is able to host the workloads","A tabletop exercise in which participants estimate each task's duration"],a:0,e:["Correct. Only a measured test shows whether recovery completes within the objective.","Incorrect. Review improves accuracy of the plan but does not measure recovery duration.","Incorrect. Attestations describe capability, but the organization's own recovery time must be measured.","Incorrect. Estimates may be optimistic, and a tabletop does not exercise real systems."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"A data center installs a generator, but a test shows servers crash in the 15 seconds before it starts and takes the load. What is missing?",c:["Additional fuel storage to extend the generator runtime beyond 72 hours","A second PDU on each rack for redundant power distribution","A UPS to carry the load until the generator starts and takes over","A surge protector on the generator output to stop voltage spikes"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Runtime matters after the generator starts. The crash happens before it takes the load.","Incorrect. PDUs distribute power but do not store energy to cover the gap.","Correct. Batteries bridge the transfer time between outage and generator power.","Incorrect. Surge protection guards against spikes and does not supply power during the gap."]},
{o:"3.4",q:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of journaling in databases and file systems?",c:["Encrypting records at the file system level so they stay protected if the disk is removed","Recording changes so the system can recover a consistent state after a crash","Copying data to a second region to provide geographic redundancy","Reducing storage consumption by compressing infrequently used data"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Encryption is a separate control. Journals track changes.","Correct. The journal can be replayed or rolled back to complete or undo interrupted operations.","Incorrect. Cross-region copying is replication.","Incorrect. Compression reduces space and is unrelated to crash recovery."]},
]);
